    "pros": [
      "20 g of plant protein with only 1 g of sugar",
      "Vegan, per the label",
      "A simple way to add a greens blend to a shake"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more per tub than the plain Simple Truth plant powder",
      "The greens dose is not stated on the listing we read",
      "No third-party certification found"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Only worth the extra if you actually want the greens; otherwise buy the plain Simple Truth plant tub.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "20 g (listing)",
      "Cost per 25 g": "Check servings on the tub",
      "Third-party tested": "None found",
      "Best for": "Plant protein plus greens"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Kroger Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-kroger-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Kroger have its own protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Kroger's Simple Truth brand sells a Grass Fed Whey in vanilla and chocolate, a regular whey, a Plant Based Protein in vanilla, chocolate and coconut, a Protein with Greens line, and an organic zero-sugar plant powder. The grass-fed whey is the best value, at about $1.65 per 25 g of protein at the $19.99 price listed on Kroger.com."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Simple Truth protein powder third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We found no third-party certification for any Simple Truth protein powder. The listings show claims such as gluten-free, non-GMO and vegan, but no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice mark, and we did not find the powders in those databases. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified name brand instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in Simple Truth protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Grass Fed Whey gives 19 g of protein in a 24 g serving, per the label record we found. The regular Simple Truth Whey lists 18 g, and the Plant Based and Protein with Greens powders list 20 g per serving. Figures vary by flavor, so check the panel on your tub."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder at Kroger?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For value, Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey, at about $1.65 per 25 g. For tested athletes, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard in a flavor listed in NSF's Certified for Sport registry. For dairy-free shoppers, the Simple Truth Plant Based tub on price, or Orgain if you want USDA Organic."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Kroger protein powder cheaper than Walmart or Costco?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not by the gram. Costco's Kirkland whey costs under $1 per 25 g and Aldi's Elevation whey about $1.00 to $1.20 when in stock, against about $1.65 for Kroger's best house whey. Kroger is competitive on name brands during sales and costs nothing extra if you already shop there."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Simple Truth plant protein vegan?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, per its listings. The Simple Truth Plant Based Protein is made from pea protein and is labeled vegan and free of gluten, dairy and soy, and the Protein with Greens tubs are labeled vegan as well. Check the sweetener on your flavor, because sources disagreed on whether it uses monk fruit or stevia."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I buy protein powder with EBT at Kroger?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some Orgain powders were marked SNAP EBT eligible on Kroger listings. Eligibility depends on whether a product is labeled as food with a Nutrition Facts panel or as a supplement with a Supplement Facts panel. Kroger states eligibility on each product page, so check there for the exact tub."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder Rankings",
    tags: [
      "kroger protein powder",
      "best kroger protein powder",
      "simple truth protein powder",
      "simple truth whey protein",
      "kroger brand protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Kroger Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What Kroger and its sister banners actually stock, whether the Simple Truth house-brand powders are worth buying, and five picks ranked by what you pay for each 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#what-kroger-sells">What Kroger Sells</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#simple-truth">Simple Truth Powders</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#testing">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#banners">Kroger Banners and Apps</a>
      <a href="#vs-other-stores">Kroger vs Other Stores</a>
      <a href="#which-to-buy">Which to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best Kroger protein powder for most shoppers is Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey, Kroger's own brand: 19 g of protein in a 24 g scoop, per the label record we found, for $19.99 a 13.5 oz jar on Kroger.com, which works out to about $1.65 per 25 g of protein. It is the cheapest whey per gram we could price at Kroger, but we found no third-party certification for it. If you are drug-tested in sport, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is the better Kroger buy, and if you avoid dairy, the Simple Truth plant powder is the house option to look at.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Kroger Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kroger's own Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey is the cheapest way to buy whey at Kroger, at about $1.65 per 25 g of protein when it is on the shelf at $19.99. It has a short, lean label and a grass-fed claim, but no third-party certification we could find, so it suits recreational lifters who want value. If you want a powder with an independent sport certification, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is the Kroger pick, at about $1.55 to $1.70 per 25 g depending on the week's price.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder (size at Kroger)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Kroger price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey (13.5 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.65</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">ON Gold Standard 100% Whey (1.5 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$29.99-31.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.55-1.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Truth Plant Based (21.6 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$24.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.80 (estimate)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Protein (about 1 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$21.99-23.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.60-2.85</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Truth Protein with Greens (18.5 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$23.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Servings not shown</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices in the table are the prices shown on Kroger.com and Kroger-banner listings in November 2026. They vary by store, by banner and by week, and Kroger runs digital coupons that can cut a tub by several dollars. Protein figures are per the label or listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor. The cost section further down shows how each number was worked out, so you can redo it with the price at your own store.
          </p>

          <p>
            One warning before you shop. Several of the Kroger listings we checked showed the item out of stock, or showed pickup and delivery as unavailable at the store selected. Kroger's protein range is real, but it is patchy store to store. The Kroger app shows stock for your chosen store, and it is worth a minute before you drive over for a particular tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you came here wondering whether Kroger is a good place to buy protein powder at all, the honest answer is that it is a convenient place, not the cheapest. Warehouse clubs and Aldi beat it on price per gram, as the store comparison below shows. Kroger wins when you are already there for groceries and want either the house whey or a certified name brand without a separate trip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-kroger-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Protein Powder Does Kroger Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kroger sells a mid-sized protein shelf that mixes its own Simple Truth powders with the mainstream sports and natural brands. Kroger is the largest traditional supermarket company in the US, and the same product pages appear across its regional banners, including Ralphs, Fred Meyer, King Soopers, Fry's, Smith's, Harris Teeter, QFC, Food 4 Less and Baker's. That matters because a powder listed on one banner's site is usually orderable at the others, even if your local store does not keep it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The house brand is Simple Truth, Kroger's natural and organic label. We found five Simple Truth protein powders on Kroger-family listings: Grass Fed Whey in vanilla and chocolate, a regular Simple Truth Whey in vanilla and chocolate, a Plant Based Protein in vanilla, chocolate and coconut, a Protein with Greens line in vanilla and chocolate, and a Simple Truth Organic Plant Based Zero Sugar powder. The section below goes through what is in each.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the name-brand side, the listings we checked showed Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey in several flavors and sizes, from 1.5 lb tubs up to 5 lb, and a wide Orgain range, including the Organic Protein in Vanilla Bean, Creamy Chocolate Fudge, Peanut Butter, Fruity Cereal and Natural Unsweetened. We also found Levels Grass Fed Whey, Garden of Life's grass-fed whey and Designer Whey on Kroger listings, and our reviews of <a href="/levels-protein-powder-review/">Levels</a> and <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life</a> cover their labels in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Kroger does not do well is the specialist end of the market. We did not find big 5 lb bags of budget whey, mass gainers in any depth, or the direct-to-consumer clean-label brands. Kroger does own Vitacost, the online supplement retailer, which carries a far larger range and ships to your door, so a Kroger shopper who wants something specific may find it there rather than in store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders at Kroger
          </h2>

          <p>
            These five powders all appeared on Kroger.com or a Kroger-banner store listing when we checked in November 2026. They are ranked by value first (cost per 25 g of protein at the listed price), then by label quality and testing. Prices are snapshots that vary by store, banner and week, and several listings showed an item out of stock at the store we checked. Label figures are per the label or listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="simple-truth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Kroger Have Its Own Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Kroger's Simple Truth brand covers both whey and plant protein, and the best of the range is the Grass Fed Whey. The 13.5 oz vanilla jar was listed at $19.99 on Kroger.com, with one banner showing $21.99. The nutrition record we found for that UPC lists a 24 g serving with 19 g of protein, about 100 calories and 2 g of sugar. That makes it roughly 79 percent protein by weight, which is in the same lean range as good name-brand wheys and leaner than most store-brand wheys we have checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The regular Simple Truth Whey, sold in vanilla and chocolate, is a separate product. Its listing says each scoop has 18 g of protein in about 90 calories with less than 1 g of sugar. We could not read a full ingredient list or a consistent shelf price for it, so we have not ranked it. If your store has it and not the grass-fed jar, compare the two cost per 25 g figures using the sum in the next section before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Simple Truth Plant Based Protein is a pea-protein powder in 21.6 oz tubs. The listings say each scoop has 20 g of protein, 6 g of fiber and 6 mg of iron, and that it is vegan and free of gluten, dairy and soy. One ingredient list we saw named monk fruit extract as a sweetener, but the list was cut off and another source named stevia, so check your tub if you avoid either. Buyer ratings on the listings were below three stars on average, and one reviewer complained the tub was half empty, which is common with powders that settle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Protein with Greens line is the same idea with a greens blend added, at 20 g of protein and 1 g of sugar a serving, per its listing, for $23.99 a tub. The Simple Truth Organic Plant Based Zero Sugar powder adds organic certification. We could not read full panels for either, which is why neither is a top pick. If you are choosing between plant powders more broadly, our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide compares the main options on protein per dollar.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HOUSE-BRAND VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey is Kroger's best value, with no certification we could find.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy it for everyday shakes if you are not drug-tested. Skip it if you compete in tested sport, or if your store only has it at full price while Gold Standard is on sale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Does Protein Powder Cost at Kroger per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At Kroger's listed prices, protein powder costs about $1.55 to $2.85 per 25 g of protein, depending on the tub. The grass-fed Simple Truth jar and a discounted Gold Standard tub sit at the low end, and small tubs of organic plant protein sit at the top. We use cost per 25 g because 25 g is about one solid serving of protein, and because it is the only number that compares a 13.5 oz jar fairly with a 5 lb tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sum is short. Work out the total protein in the container, divide the price by it, and multiply by 25. For the Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey, a 383 g jar with a 24 g serving holds about 16 servings, so about 304 g of protein. $19.99 divided by 304, times 25, is about $1.65. At the $21.99 price on another banner, the same jar is about $1.81 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Servings x protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein in tub</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey (13.5 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 16 x 19 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 304 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.65</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">ON Gold Standard (1.5 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 x 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 480 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$29.99-31.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.55-1.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Truth Plant Based (21.6 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 17 x 20 g (estimate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 340 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$24.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.80</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Protein (1.02 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10 x 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 210 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$21.99-23.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.60-2.85</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The plant powder figure is an estimate, and we want to be plain about why. The Kroger listings we could read gave 20 g of protein per scoop and a 21.6 oz (about 612 g) tub, but not the serving weight or the number of servings. If the scoop weighs about 35 to 36 g, which is typical for a 20 g pea-protein serving, the tub holds about 17 servings and the cost is about $1.80 per 25 g at $24.99. The real figure is on the side of the tub, and it takes ten seconds to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things move these numbers more than brand choice. The first is size: Gold Standard in a 5 lb tub works out cheaper per gram than the 1.5 lb tub, when your store has it and the price is reasonable. The second is Kroger's digital coupons and weekly sales, which regularly take a few dollars off a tub. A $5 coupon on the 1.5 lb Gold Standard tub cuts about 26 cents off every 25 g of protein. For the wider picture of what protein costs across stores and formats, see <a href="/how-much-does-protein-powder-cost/">how much protein powder costs</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Kroger Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We found no third-party certification for any Simple Truth protein powder. The listings carry claims such as gluten-free, non-GMO and vegan, and the plant powder lists Kroger's own "Live Naturally" tag, but none of the Simple Truth tubs we checked showed an NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice mark, and we did not find them in those programs' public databases. That does not mean the powders are unsafe. It means nobody outside the brand has publicly checked them for banned substances or label accuracy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among the name brands Kroger stocks, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is the one with a strong testing record: several Gold Standard flavors are listed in NSF's Certified for Sport registry, which tests for hundreds of banned substances and checks that the label matches the contents. Garden of Life's Sport line also claims NSF Certified for Sport on its listings. Orgain is USDA Organic, which is about how ingredients are farmed and handled, not a test for contaminants or banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a separate question from sport testing. Plant proteins and chocolate flavors tend to carry more lead and cadmium than vanilla whey, because pea, rice, seeds and cacao take metals up from soil. None of the Simple Truth listings published heavy metal results that we could find. If that is your main worry, choose a vanilla whey, and read our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> for brands that publish test data.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are drug-tested in sport, the rule is simple: buy only a product and flavor you can find in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database on the day you buy it, and keep the lot number. At Kroger that means Gold Standard in a listed flavor, not the house brand. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists the certified options across all stores.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="banners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Kroger Banners, the App and Online Prices
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kroger runs one product catalog across its banners, so the same Simple Truth UPC shows up on Kroger.com, Ralphs.com, FredMeyer.com and the rest, often at slightly different prices. In our checks the grass-fed whey was $19.99 on Kroger.com and $21.99 on Baker's, and the plant powder was $24.99 on one banner and $22.99 on sale on another. That spread is normal for regional pricing and is worth knowing if you live near two banners.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Kroger app is the most reliable way to see the price at your own store, along with any digital coupon you need to clip before checkout. Delivery orders can carry higher prices or fees than the shelf, and some protein listings showed delivery unavailable, so pickup or an in-store trip is usually the cheapest route for a tub. Fuel points earned on groceries are a small extra that a store-brand-only shopper does not get at a warehouse club.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some Orgain listings were marked as SNAP EBT eligible, which surprises people. Eligibility depends on how the product is labeled: powders sold with a Nutrition Facts panel as food can qualify, while products labeled with a Supplement Facts panel generally cannot. If you shop with EBT, the Kroger listing states eligibility item by item, so check there rather than assuming a whole brand qualifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, Vitacost. Kroger bought the online retailer in 2014, and it carries far more protein brands than any Kroger store, including most of the certified and clean-label brands we review. It is a separate site with its own prices and shipping rules, but for a Kroger loyalist who wants something the store does not stock, it is the obvious next stop before a general marketplace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-other-stores" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Kroger Cheaper Than Walmart, Aldi or Costco for Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kroger is rarely the cheapest place to buy protein powder by the gram. Aldi's Elevation whey has sold for about $1.00 to $1.20 per 25 g when it is in stock, and Costco's Kirkland whey runs under $1 per 25 g at warehouse prices, as our <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/">Aldi</a> and <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">Costco</a> guides show. Walmart carries bigger tubs of budget name brands that usually beat Kroger's small jars on price per gram, which our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">Walmart protein powder</a> guide covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Kroger holds up is in the middle. At about $1.65 per 25 g, Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey is cheaper than most name-brand grass-fed wheys at any grocery store, and Gold Standard on a Kroger sale is close to what other supermarkets charge. Against another regional chain, Meijer's own whey works out a little cheaper per gram, about $1.15 per 25 g, as our <a href="/best-meijer-protein-powder/">Meijer protein powder</a> guide shows, but with more fillers in each scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Membership clubs need their fee counted. A Costco or Sam's Club card costs money every year, and if protein is the only thing you would buy there, the saving per tub has to cover it. A Kroger shopper who already buys groceries there pays nothing extra to pick up a jar, and that convenience is a fair reason to pay 30 to 60 cents more per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other comparison is online. Amazon and brand sites often undercut grocery shelves on large tubs of name brands, especially during sales. If you use one powder steadily and have room for a 5 lb tub, ordering online and keeping a Kroger jar as a backup is the cheapest pattern for most people. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide ranks the lowest cost per gram anywhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Kroger Protein Powder Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For everyday shakes on a budget, buy Simple Truth Grass Fed Whey. It is the cheapest whey per gram at Kroger, its label is lean, and the grass-fed claim and lack of artificial flavors appeal to shoppers who would otherwise pay more for a premium brand. Accept that you are trusting the label without outside testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete in tested sport, or you simply want independent checks behind your powder, buy Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard in a flavor listed in NSF's registry, and buy the biggest tub your store has when it is on sale. It is the only widely stocked powder at Kroger with that level of documentation, and on a good week it is barely more expensive per gram than the house brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you avoid dairy, start with the Simple Truth Plant Based tub for price, but check the servings count and reviews first, because buyer ratings were weak. If organic certification matters more than price, Orgain is the organic pick and is easy to find across banners, and our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> goes through its flavors and sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want something Kroger does not stock, such as an isolate with a sport certification or a powder sweetened without stevia, look at Vitacost or our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings rather than settling for what is on the shelf. A powder you will drink every day is worth getting right.
          </p>
        </div>

